  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 1210 AM MDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nCurrent radar imagery shows a few sprinkles lingering over the \nDenver metro at this time, with patchy low clouds visible on \nsatellite. This evening was quite active as storms that formed \nover the southern Front Range and Palmer Divide became clusters of\nheavy rainfall, leading to the issuance of a Flood Advisory and \none Flash Flood Warning. This goes to show that the ingredients \nnecessary for efficient rainfall production are already present in\nour atmosphere, and they will remain present through Thursday \nnight, hence the high PoPs over the next 48 hours. However, the \ntricky part about this forecast will be pinning down when the best\ninstability or forcing will materialize and therefore, the \nheaviest rainfall rates.\n\nMoisture continues to increase Wednesday afternoon and we expect \nshower coverage to become more widespread. Convection will likely \nbegin over the mountains first at around 12PM-1PM, while the Denver \nmetro and east Colorado plains would start to see a better chance \nfor downpours by mid afternoon. The best window for heavy rainfall \nover the Denver metro looks to be between 4PM and 8PM Wednesday \nevening while instability is at its highest (~500 J/kg CAPE). The \neast Colorado plains may see the peak of rainfall activity occur \nlater in the evening as the upper level disturbance and vorticity \nmaxima push eastwards. Slow storm motion vectors (10-15 mph) and \ntraining may lead to continued rainfall over the same location, and \nat least a localized flash flooding threat. Areas that have already \nreceived rainfall from storms the last couple of days (mainly the \nPalmer Divide) will be more vulnerable to flooding given increased \nsoil moisture already in place. \n\nThe nature of the showers will become more stratiform-like after \n10PM Wednesday night, with scattered showers continuing through most \nof the day on Thursday given such anomalous moisture present (1.0-\n1.4\" PWATS). Thick low level clouds will limit instability and keep \nafternoon temperatures at low elevations 10-15 degrees below normal, \nwith some areas only reaching the mid 60s. Shower chances decrease \nThursday night as flow aloft becomes slightly more zonal and the \ndisturbance completely moves off into the Great Plains. If high \nclouds are able to clear Thursday night, leftover moisture is likely \nto lead to the development of some areas of fog, particularly \nover the plains. Additional showers will be possible with a \nshortwave Friday afternoon, mainly over the high terrain. \n\nSaturday and Sunday look to be dry and warmer, with afternoon \ntemperatures warming back up to the low 80s for the Denver metro. \nModels are trending cooler for next week due to the passage of an \nupper level trough sometime between Monday-Tuesday.",
